Z||000000||80's BOY||08-09-2000||02:30 PM||||i saw a convertible something yesterday, and was a triumph t-7 i think. has anyone ever seen one of these? what kind of engine does it have in the back?? almost looked like a fiero at first.||||reg||5
Z||000001||286SE'S||08-09-2000||02:56 PM||||Our janitor had one about 10 years ago and was always using it as an excuse to not come to work. As cool as the TR7 looks it is not a very well made car. My brother-in-law is a real British car fanatic and calls them the "worst thing Triumph ever made. I always check them out when I see one. The engine is in the front and looks pretty small. I love the interior, great dashboard layout and long windshield,  more went into the looks than the construction. The front suspension parts look so frail compared to a Fieros. Don't see many on the road anymore, even in summer.<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||08-09-2000||03:18 PM||||The TR-7 was a fun car.. and has a pretty decent race history. the Shop that takes care of my car supports 2 in SCCA IT-A. Scott Elliot (7) purple car.. hasn't placed less than 4th in his class in the 3 years he driven the car. Parts are WAY more scarse than Fiero parts.. Rimmer makes a supercharger kit for them. weber still makes a dual carb kit for them. Kirk Racing, Carrera and Power slot make stuff for the car. they can be fun but really hard to make fast. yeah they'll run 130.. but so can an 84 Cressida. many have been fitted with the rover/buick 3.5liter V8. that helps make it quick but still not close to vett or V8 Fiero numbers. If you interested in Triumphs go to <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>  and click on the link to Grassroots Motorsports web page. they have a 12 second rotary Spitfire!! and the makers of Johns broken kitty (Chevy V8 swap in to a Jag) they make a kit to put a Ford 302 into a Triumph TR-3A... a car the same size as the Tr-7 with a lot less weight and a targa top... <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||webbee||08-09-2000||03:35 PM||||They also made a TR-8 which is the TR-7 with an aluminum V-8. It goes fast. As I remember the V-8 was from Rover. This was originally the GM 1962 Buick Special, all aluminum V-8, that was sold to Rover by GM.<P>This engine might be an interesting swap into a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||08-09-2000||03:42 PM|||| when they came out they only produced 137hp! out of a 3.5l V8. in full race trim they only belt out about 360hp.. and in full race trim the TR-8 tips the scales at 21-200lbs.  <A HREF="http://canadianwebs.com/wedgeparts/scanned2.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://canadianwebs.com/wedgeparts/scanned2.htm</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||08-10-2000||02:24 AM||||1FSTGT--The TR3A was a rag top, not a targa.  Here's a pic of a '59 TR3.  The A was just a slightly (very slightly) upgraded version of the 3.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/'59_TR3.jpg"> <P>I can't think of any Triumphs that had a targa top.  Are you maybe thinking of the Fiat (Bertone) X19.  Similar in appearance to the TR7 & 8, but with a targa???||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||08-10-2000||03:50 AM||||Having someone say that the TR-7 was the worst car Triumph ever made was a real slam considering I owned a 1970 GT-6.  Fun car but there wasn't a piece of rubber on that car that didn't need replacing every two years.  I did brake rebuilds year after year.  If you think working on a Fiero takes up all your time, you definitely do not want to get into the English cars.||||reg||1
